```

### Alternative: find max first, then max of the rest

```java
Optional<BigDecimal> maxSalary = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getSalary)
    .max(Comparator.naturalOrder());

maxSalary.ifPresent(max -> {
    Optional<BigDecimal> secondMax = employees.stream()
        .map(Employee::getSalary)
        .filter(s -> s.compareTo(max) < 0)
        .max(Comparator.naturalOrder());
    // secondMax is the second-highest salary
});
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: forgetting distinct and getting the same salary twice.

```java
// WRONG: without distinct, if two employees earn Rs.1800000,
// skip(1) gives Rs.1800000 again, not Rs.1500000
Optional<BigDecimal> wrong = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getSalary)
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1)
    .findFirst();

// CORRECT: distinct collapses duplicate amounts
Optional<BigDecimal> correct = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getSalary)
    .distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1)
    .findFirst();
```

### What about finding second-highest per department?

```java
Map<String, Optional<BigDecimal>> secondPerDept = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Employee::getDepartment,
        Collectors.collectingAndThen(
            Collectors.mapping(Employee::getSalary,
                Collectors.toList()),
            salaries -> salaries.stream()
                .distinct()
                .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
                .skip(1)
                .findFirst())));
```

### What are the edge cases?

```java
// All employees earn the same: second-highest is empty
// Single employee: second-highest is empty
// Two employees same salary: distinct collapses to one, second is empty
```

### What about returning all employees at the second-highest salary?

The two-step approach handles this: find the amount first, then filter all employees matching it. This correctly returns multiple employees when ties exist at the second-highest level.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What if all employees earn the same salary?" The second-highest does not exist; findFirst returns empty.
- "How do you generalize to Nth-highest?" Change skip(1) to skip(n-1).
- "How do you find the second-highest per department?" Group first, then apply the pattern within each group.
